[mali-g2] Release command buffer memory now and then, and two small per-call leaks.

The Mali driver keeps host memory for every render pass instance (about 420 bytes per
vkCmdBeginRendering) recorded into a command buffer that also records a query (timestamp or
occlusion), until that command buffer is reset with RELEASE_RESOURCES or its pool is destroyed;
resets with flags 0 never give it back. DXVK resets its command pools with flags 0 and records the
game's D3D11 queries into the same command buffer as its rendering, so a Unity game's server grew by
~85 MB a minute until Scudo could no longer map memory (abort in malloc under vkBeginCommandBuffer).
The server now adds RELEASE_RESOURCES to every VORTEK_CMD_RELEASE_EVERY-th (default 32, 1 = always,
0 = never) vkResetCommandPool / vkResetCommandBuffer of the same pool or command buffer (a 64-entry
table per connection; a new or colliding handle releases). Implicit resets by vkBeginCommandBuffer
are not covered. Test: tests/frame_leak_test.c, tests/frame_leak_test.sh.

vkWaitSemaphores (timeline_semaphore.c) and the async pipeline creation unserialize into a
MemoryPool without a backing buffer, and vt_free() frees the chunks but not the allocation list's
array: 32 bytes leaked per call.

